integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/state.json" "file_sha256" "9271e52d31e0b6b012a34f18bc1103d64ceb6c2e038f1fe49e0407440395290f"} {"question" "q-88689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "719d109e0f263a23944557d97e05a7591b3925e13f943ab431df7e88d5f39d5f"} {"question" "q-88689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/state.json" "file_sha256" "9271e52d31e0b6b012a34f18bc1103d64ceb6c2e038f1fe49e0407440395290f"} {"question" "q-88689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["